This division was not solely linguistic; the divide was also jurisdictional and ecclesiastical. The [[diocesan]] divisions are nearly identical to the dialectal divisions. Old West Frisian, largely coterminous with the [[Diocese of Utrecht (695–1580)|Diocese of Utrecht]], was divided into two dialects: the southwestern dialect in and around [[Westergoa]] and the northeastern dialect in and around [[Eastergoa]]. These dialects later developed into a continuum after [[Middelzee|the sea arm which divided them]] began to be [[Land reclamation|reclaimed]] around 1100.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=109}} Old East Frisian was divided twice as well: Old Weser Frisian in the [[Diocese of Bremen]] and Old {{ill|Ems Frisian|nl|Eemsfries|fy|Iemsfrysk}} in the [[Diocese of Münster]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=109}} During the period of Old Frisian, the dialect which later became [[North Frisian language|North Frisian]] is not attested.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=109}} Stiles states that both varieties of North Frisian – Insular and Mainland – are ultimately descended from an Eastern Frisian ancestor.{{sfn|Stiles|2008|p=176}}

The descendants of Old Weser Frisian – also known as [[Rüstringen|Riustring]] Old East Frisian – are [[Wangerooge Frisian|Wangerooge]], [[Wursten Frisian|Wursten]], and [[Harlingerland Frisian]], all of which are now [[Language death|extinct]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=111}}{{sfn|Bremmer|2001|p=603}} Old Weser Frisian is attested in two full manuscripts, known as the Riustring Codices, and two fragments.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=111}}{{sfn|Bremmer|2001|p=603}} Whether the Old Weser Frisian attested in these documents is the direct ancestor of the Wangerooge or Wursten variants or rather an extremely close relative is a matter of some debate; Stiles argues that the documents' language is closely related to the two but distinct from them, while Bremmer categorizes them as direct descendants.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=111}}{{sfn|Stiles|2008|pp=176–177}} Old Ems Frisian is the ancestor of the now-extinct Emsingo, [[Brokmerland]], and {{lang|nl|[[Ommelanden]]}} dialects, as well as the still-extant [[Saterland Frisian language|Saterland Frisian]], its only living descendant.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=113}}{{sfn|Bremmer|2001|p=603}} Old West Frisian later developed into the modern [[West Frisian language]].{{sfn|Winter|2022|p=6}}

Unlike their [[Mainland North Frisian languages|Mainland counterparts]], the [[Insular North Frisian languages]] are not descended from Old Frisian. Instead, they share a common ancestor in Pre–Old Frisian, diverging around the late 7th or early 8th centuries.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=6}}

