Traditionally, [[English language|English]] and the Frisian languages were widely regarded as closer to each other than to any other Germanic language.{{sfn|Stiles|2018|p=1}} The German linguist [[Theodor Siebs]] is commonly associated with popularizing this affinity and is credited with coining the term "[[Anglo-Frisian languages]]" in his 1889 dissertation entitled {{lang|de|Zur Geschichte der Englisch-friesischen Sprache}} ('On the History of the Anglo-Frisian Languages'). The English philologist [[Henry Sweet]], however, is considered the "father of the Anglo-Frisian hypothesis", articulating the concept as early as 1876.<ref>{{multiref2|For Sweet as the progenitor of the hypothesis, see {{harvnb|Kaiser|2021|pp=31–32}}.|For the earliest mentions by Sweet referencing the hypothesis and Siebs's influence on the concept, see {{harvnb|Stiles|2018|pp=1, 3}}.|For everything else, see {{harvnb|Versloot|2021|p=339}}.}}</ref> Observations about the close relationship are much older than the 19th century, however; it is likely that [[Anglo-Saxon mission]]aries during the 7th and 8th centuries saw the two languages as closely related.{{sfn|Versloot|2021|p=339}} Datings proposed for a common ancestor of the Anglo-Frisian languages estimate that it was probably fully formed by the 4th or 5th century, diverging shortly thereafter.{{sfn|Schrijver|2017|p=48}}

This phylogenetic view of English and Frisian is no longer widely accepted.{{sfn|Buczek|2020|pp=245, 270}} Linguists argue that – while English, Frisian, and Low German are correctly believed to have a common Ingvaeonic ancestor{{snd}}there is no reason to believe that English and Frisian shared a uniquely close genetic relationship thereafter.<ref>{{harvnb|Bazelmans|2009|p=326}}: "According to most researchers, this means that there cannot have been an 'original' Anglo-Frisian entity [...]."</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Stiles|2018|p=31}}: "It is not possible to construct the exclusive common relative chronology that is necessary in order to be able to establish a node on a family tree. The term and concept of 'Anglo-Frisian' should be banished to the historiography of the subject."</ref> Some shared linguistic changes do overlap in ways unique to these languages, often at similar times, but these changes do not match in terms of their relative chronology; in other words, these common changes do not appear to have occurred at the same time or in the same order.{{sfnm|1a1=Versloot|1y=2021|1pp=341–342|2a1=Stiles|2y=2018|2pp=5–6|3a1=Schrijver|3y=2017|3p=48}} Examples include Old Frisian's [[vowel breaking]] and [[Vowel backness|vowel backing]] processes, which closely resemble Old English's and Old Norse's, but developed independently from them.{{sfn|Kleiner|2025|p=170}} Instead, some linguists argue that the Ingvaeonic precursor was likely a broad [[dialect continuum]] which saw the dialects which later became English and Frisian [[Wave theory|develop similarly but not as one language]]. This continuum was spoken across the continental coast of the North Sea prior to the [[Migration Period]], evolving into distinct languages around the turn of the 5th century.{{sfn|Bazelmans|2009|pp=325–326}} The continuum model is sometimes broadened to include [[Old Low Franconian]] as well.{{sfn|Buczek|2020|p=251}} Under this model, the two language groups did experience a series of changes [[Sprachbund|particular to the area]] along the North Sea between about 450 and 650, which influenced both languages as well as Dutch, [[Flemish dialects|Flemish]], and probably northern varieties of Low German.{{sfn|Versloot|2021|p=346}}

The English and the Frisians were long associated with each other. Frisians are traditionally believed to have comprised a fairly significant portion of [[Anglo-Saxon settlement of Britain|the Germanic invaders of Britain]] during the [[Anglo-Saxon]] period. While no major district of England is named after the Frisians, there is data from the local [[toponymy]] – that is, placename origins – to support a significant Frisian settlement, including [[Friston]] and [[Frisby, Leicestershire|Frisby]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=125, 127}} Genetic evidence has suggested that following the Roman-era exodus of the [[Frisii]], the people who later inhabited the area were genetically indistinguishable from the 5th-century [[Angles (tribe)|Angles]] who colonized what is now England.<ref>{{harvnb|Colleran|2019|p=78}}: "The Anglo-Frisian ancestor suggested by the neogrammarians <!-- sic --> is a historical reality, at least in a genetic sense. The Frisians really are Angles."</ref> Frisian and English domination of maritime trade in the North Sea also played a role in their relationship; London was a hub for Frisian [[slave-traders]] and [[York]] had a special [[quarter (urban subdivision)|quarter]] for housing Frisian merchants.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=128}} Although the Anglo-Saxons also [[Anglo-Saxon settlement in the Netherlands|invaded and subjugated the Frisians during the 5th century]], this is not considered to be a cause for the linguistic similarities.{{sfn|Bazelmans|2009|pp=321, 326}}

Other scholars, however, have persisted in supporting the Anglo-Frisian language family as a legitimate phylogenetic category, split into two general outlooks on the relationship. The first is the traditional model, which contends that the relationship is that the two languages diverged from a common Proto-Anglo-Frisian ancestor and thus are [[sister language]]s.{{sfn|Buczek|2020|pp=249–250}} The other is called the convergence hypothesis, which regards Ingvaeonic as the last common ancestor, but holds that early forms of English and Frisian became increasingly intertwined and influenced by each other to form the striking resemblance each shares to the other.{{sfn|Buczek|2020|p=251}} Critics dismiss the convergence approach as unrealistic, pointing to the difficulty of dispersing those kinds of linguistic developments across the maritime divide.{{sfn|Buczek|2020|p=252}} The Danish linguist [[Hans Frede Nielsen|Hans F. Nielsen]] placed Old Saxon and Old Frisian closer on his phylogenetic tree, with Old English splitting off in the 5th century not long after Old High German split from the rest of the West Germanic languages.{{sfn|Kaiser|2021|p=29}}

==History==
===Speakers===
{{broader|History of the Frisians}}
[[File:Schilderwerk huldiging Friezen - Münster - 20317896 - RCE (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright=1.75|A reproduction of a wall painting at [[Münster Cathedral]] showing Frisians offering gifts to the cathedral's patron|alt=A black-and-white drawing of four groups of people flanking a man with a halo and a sword; the four groups comprise women and men who are bringing offerings of livestock and agriculture; one man in each group is armed with a spear; above the piece is an inscription in Latin]]
During the [[early Middle Ages]], when Pre–Old Frisian was spoken, the [[Frisians]] dominated North Sea trade. Contemporary non-Frisian documents refer to the North Sea as the Frisian Sea ({{langx|la|Mare Frisicum}}) and the term ''Frisian'' was used in [[Dorestad]] to mean any merchant, not necessarily an ethnic Frisian.<ref>{{multiref|{{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=2}}.|{{harvnb|Looijenga|2003|pp=69–70}}.|{{harvnb|Nijdam|Hallebeek|de Jong|2023|p=4}}.}}</ref> By the early 7th century, the Frisians had expanded from the {{ill|Sincfal|nl}} near modern-day [[Bruges]] to the [[Weser]] estuary.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=2}}{{sfn|Knol|2021|p=13}} During the latter half of the century, the first wave of Frisians began colonizing the previously uninhabited islands off the southwestern coast of modern-day Denmark, occupying [[Amrum]], [[Föhr]], [[Sylt]], and [[Heligoland]]; the linguistic descendants of this migration are the Insular North Frisian speakers, who speak [[Öömrang]], [[Fering]], [[Söl'ring]], and [[Heligoland Frisian|Halunder Frisian]] varieties, respectively.{{sfn|Winter|2022|p=11}}{{efn|Fering and Öömrang are sometimes considered to be one dialect.{{sfn|Winter|2022|p=11}}}} By the end of the century, the Frisians also controlled the coastal regions from the [[Scheldt]] to the [[Rhine]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=2}} During the following period, Christianity was introduced to the region by the Anglo-Saxon bishop [[Willibrord]] and Frisia was subjugated by the [[Francia|Franks]] under the leadership of [[Charles Martel]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=2}} Charles Martel's incursion into Frisia is probably what led to the departure of the Insular North Frisians from Frisia to the uninhabited islands off the coast of southern Denmark.{{sfn|Bremmer|2001|p=602}}

[[File:Lauwers.svg|thumb|left|The [[Lauwers]] in the modern-day Netherlands]]

During the latter part of the 13th century{{snd}}when Old Frisian was spoken{{snd}}the Frisians were divided by the Lauwers river. Those to the west of it were partially conquered by the [[County of Holland]] during [[Friso-Hollandic Wars|its long-standing campaigns of conquest]], but they were ultimately able to repel Holland's forces, killing [[William IV of Holland|its count]] at the [[Battle of Warns]] in 1345.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=3}} The political situation east of the river is largely obscure during this period, but it appears that they were under regular assault from [[Duchy of Saxony|Saxon]] forces though they were able to keep them at bay.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=3}} This period is also marked by a loose confederation between the Frisian territories, the [[Upstalsboom|Upstalsboom League]], which united the [[Seven Sealands]] of Frisia and produced legal documents from around 1300, though translations of its original Latin texts only appear in Old West Frisian.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=3–4}} However, [[Vetkopers and Schieringers|an internal rift among the Frisian confederacy]] increased tensions and ultimately led to the end of the [[Frisian freedom]] period in 1498, with the ascension of [[Albert III of Saxony]] as {{lang|la|[[Potestaat of Friesland|gubernator]]}}.{{sfn|Nijdam|Hallebeek|de Jong|2023|p=9}}{{sfn|Hoekstra|2003|p=195}} The following centuries were marked by [[civil war]]s including the [[Guelders Wars]], which saw more Frisian casualties than any war thereafter.{{sfn|Mol|2022|p=13}}

===Corpus===
[[File:Second Hunsingo Codex (H2), page 7.jpg|thumb|The {{ill|Hunsingo Statutes|fy|Hunzinger Lânrjocht|nl|Hunsingoër Landrecht|v=ib}}, originally formulated in 1252, is one of the oldest surviving Old Frisian texts.{{sfn|Johnston|2001|p=580}} This copy dates to about 1350. <!-- source in image file --> |alt=An old manuscript with thick blackletter font with some blue- and red-colored writing in some spots]]
Outside of the two dozen surviving [[Anglo-Saxon runes|Pre–Old Frisian runic inscriptions]] – all of which are dated to around the 5th through 10th centuries – and some individual words captured in the [[marginalia]] of Latin texts, the earliest surviving Frisian-language text is an [[interlinear gloss]] of the [[Psalms]] found in 2015, which has been dated to around 1100.<ref>{{multiref2|For the number and dating of runic inscriptions, see {{harvnb|Kaiser|2021|p=13}}.|For everything else, see {{harvnb|Nijdam|Hallebeek|de Jong|2023|pp=11–12}}.}}</ref> The first full manuscripts are the First Brokmer Codex, written sometime between 1276 and 1300, and the First Riustring Codex, written around 1300. These documents are known to be copies and it is uncertain when, where, or by whom the original texts were written, though it is likely that they were originally composed shortly after 1225.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=6–7}} 

Legal texts dominate the surviving [[Text corpus|corpus]] of Old Frisian documents; all but one of the Frisian-language documents east of the Lauwers are legal documents.<ref>{{multiref2|For the overwhelming majority being legal documents, see {{harvnb|Nedoma|2017|p=882}}, {{harvnb|Bremmer|2023|p=95}}, {{harvnb|Versloot|2023|p=128}}, {{harvnb|Johnston|2001|p=571}}, {{harvnb|Hoekstra|2003|p=193}}, and {{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=7}}.|For documents east of the Lauwers, see {{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=7}}.}}</ref> To the west, textual diversity is somewhat wider. Western documents include over a thousand [[charter]]s and administrative documents, though poetry and historiographies have survived alongside them as well as several religious works and a few administrative texts.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=8}}{{sfn|Hoekstra|2003|p=193}} Most of the existing corpora were compiled into seventeen legal codices, one being an [[incunable]], which contain several distinct legal texts.{{sfn|Johnston|2001|p=571}} Many of the codices are not fully in Old Frisian; the {{lang|la|Codex Parisiensis}} ('Parisian Codex'), for example, contains Middle Low German and Latin supplements, and the {{lang|la|Jus Municipale Frisonum}} ('Municipal Law of the Frisians') ends with a [[Middle Dutch]] legal text.{{sfn|Johnston|2001|p=575}} 

Taken together, the body of surviving Old Frisian documents is remarkably uniform across time and space, and formed an early [[standard language]] for Frisians.{{sfn|Hoekstra|2003|p=193}} In general, the legal texts from the [[High Middle Ages]] emphasize the role of upholding the [[social custom]]s and traditions of the [[peasantry]], whereas the texts of the [[late Middle Ages]] were shaped much more by the growing influence of [[Roman law]] and the [[canon law of the Catholic Church]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2023|p=96}} Later texts focus heavily on the introduction of an emerging educated class to the legal process as the capacity for urban and regional administration grew.{{sfn|Bremmer|2023|p=96}} The vast majority of Old Frisian documents, however, have not survived to the modern day and much of what had been preserved was destroyed during the [[Reformation]], especially as [[Suppression of monasteries#Netherlands|monastic orders around the Netherlands were dissolved]].{{sfn|Nijdam|Hallebeek|de Jong|2023|p=11}}

Documentation for Old Frisian is also attested in [[chronicle]]s and [[apocrypha]], most of which are politically charged and considered inaccurate; the political situation of the Frisians during this period led to ideological influences on the Old Frisian body of literature.{{sfn|Johnston|2001|p=571}} For example, the aforementioned incunable, though containing some legal content such as the [[Statutes of Upstalsboom]], begins with an ideologically-driven introduction and has several other documents which likely served to promote the [[Frisian freedom|Frisian self-governing, non-feudal social order of the period]].{{sfn|Johnston|2001|p=572}} Later works emphasize the Frisian [[legal tradition]], especially its sources and purported unbroken line from generations past.{{sfn|Murdoch|2004|p=255}}

===Decline and evolution===
During Latin's descent as the chosen language of legal texts like charters, Frisian also began its linguistic decline. [[Low German]] began to displace it, either because it was of higher [[Prestige (sociolinguistics)|prestige]] or more widely understood. However, Old Frisian documents were still widely translated into Low German from the late 15th century until the turn of the 17th century and modern Low German demonstrates traces of Old Frisian influence, including in placenames, personal names, vocabulary, and [[syntax]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=7–8}} Between the Lauwers and the Ems, no original Frisian texts occur in the record after around 1450 and the last known public document composed in Old Frisian dates to 1547 following the introduction of [[Dutch language|Dutch]] as the [[language of administration]] by the representatives of the [[Duchy of Saxony]] during the 16th century.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=7–8}}{{sfn|Hoekstra|2003|p=194}}

By 1550, the language is considered to have developed into [[Middle Frisian]], mostly as a vernacular in rural areas, or been superseded by {{lang|fy|[[Stadsfries Dutch|Stêdsk]]}}, a Frisian–Dutch [[mixed language]] used primarily in cities by Frisians who could not speak Dutch.{{sfn|Hoekstra|2003|pp=193, 195}} Documents produced after 1550 show marked differences in orthography and grammar, suggesting that during the first half of the 16th century, the Old Frisian documents being produced were already quite archaic.{{sfn|Hoekstra|2003|p=196}} During the emerging Modern Frisian period in the 19th century, Old Frisian documents were once again being consulted as inspiration for orthographic standards. Around the middle of the century, {{ill|Harmen Sytstra|fy||nl}} developed an orthography based largely on the conventions of Old Frisian documents. A variant of it vied to become the written standard for West Frisian, but lost out to an orthography developed by the [[Brothers Halbertsma]], which was based largely on the work of the Middle Frisian poet [[Gysbert Japicx]].{{sfn|Hoekstra|2003|p=198}}

==Vocabulary==
[[File:Komst van den H. Willebrord in Nederland.jpg|thumb|Old Frisian vocabulary was influenced by the Christianization of the Frisians.|alt=An illustration of a tonsured saint in a black robe holding a staff in the shape of a cross ministering at the seashore to Germanic pagans; the sky is dark except for a light break in the clouds near the saint]]
===Native vocabulary===
Although the vast majority of Old Frisian vocabulary can be traced directly to [[Proto-Germanic]], many terms were created through [[Compound word|compounding]] or [[affixation]], and were [[Loan word|borrowed from other languages]]. Only a few adverb-forming suffixes are attested; adverbs could otherwise be formed using either the [[genitive]] or [[dative case]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=91}} Nouns were regularly combined without any use of genitive forms, such as in {{lang|ofs|fiskdam}} ('fishing weir'), though it became increasingly common to mark the first element with a linking genitive form like {{lang|ofs|-s}}, such as in {{lang|ofs|sumeresnacht}} ('summer night'), in later forms of the language.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=94}} Adjectives were also compounded with nouns to form other adjectives, such as {{lang|ofs|ūdertam}} ('easy to milk', {{lit|udder-tame}}).{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=94}}

Although relatively rare, [[kenning]]s – a kind of Germanic [[compound (linguistics)|compound]] with a metaphorical meaning – are attested in some Old Frisian documents. For example, [[Criminal law|criminal regulations]] regarding the protection of children and pregnant women use the term {{lang|ofs|bēnenaburch}}{{efn|The alternative form {{lang|ofs|bēnetaburch}} is also found.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=132–133}} For the long vowel, see {{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=94}}.}} ('fortress of the bones') to reference the womb.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=130–133}} Among expressive vocabulary, more words for anger are attested than any other emotion.{{sfn|Bremmer|Neri|Schuhmann|2025|p=72}}

===Loanwords===
Loanwords in Old Frisian comprised inherited borrowings from earlier languages – such as {{lang|ofs|rīke}} ('kingdom, realm') borrowed from a [[Celtic language]] during either the Proto-Germanic or Proto–West Germanic periods – and borrowings during the Old Frisian period.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=94}} Old Frisian borrowed a number of Latin terms from all three periods and it is often difficult to pinpoint precisely when the Latin loan entered the language.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=94–95}} Old Frisian also appears to have borrowed terms from the [[Slavic languages]] through Low German, including the term {{lang|ofs|cona}} ('fur') which was used as money in [[Rüstringen|Riustring]] (compare the [[Serbo-Croatian]] term {{lang|sh|[[Croatian kuna|kuna]]}}).{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=96}} Terms from [[Old French]] were also borrowed, probably through one or more intermediaries. Examples include {{lang|ofs|payement}} ('payment') and {{lang|ofs|amīe}} ('female lover, [[concubine]]'). Old Frisian also borrowed a number of abstract suffixes from French.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=96}}

Old Saxon and Middle Low German also contributed significantly to Old Frisian vocabulary. By the end of the 15th century, Middle Low German had emerged as the [[Lingua franca|language of trade]] in the North Sea, displacing Old Frisian dialects spoken east of the Lauwers. Terms borrowed include {{lang|ofs|reth}} ('wheel'; from Old Saxon {{lang|osx|rath}}) and {{lang|ofs|swāger}} ('brother-in-law').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=95–96}} Similarly, Old Saxon and Middle Low German served as an intermediary for Old and Middle High German borrowings; these include terms like {{lang|ofs|keisere}} ('emperor'; from Old High German {{lang|goh|kaisar}}) and {{lang|ofs|iunkfrouwe}} ('young woman, virgin').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=95}}

===Effects of Christianization===
{{broader|Christianization of the Germanic peoples}}
After the Christianization of the Frisians around the 8th century, the language experienced an influx of Latin and its Greek loans, such as {{lang|ofs|diōvel}} ('devil'; from Latin {{lang|la|diabolus}}), {{lang|ofs|skrīva}} ('to write'; from Latin {{lang|la|scrībere}}, displacing the native term {{lang|ofs|wrīta}}), and {{lang|ofs|seininge}} ('blessing'; from Latin {{lang|la|signum}} '[[sign of the cross]]').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=95}} Because the [[Anglo-Saxons]] converted the Frisians to Christianity, it is probable that Old English terms began to enter the language around this time, though the close relationship between the two languages makes distinguishing native words from Old English borrowings extremely difficult. Possible borrowings may include {{lang|ofs|trachtia}} ('to yearn'; from Old English {{lang|ang|treahtian}}, 'to comment on') and {{lang|ofs|diligia}} ('to delete'; from Old English {{lang|ang|dīlegian}}, 'to blot out, to erase'), though these terms may have been borrowed from Old English to missionary centers in other Germanic-speaking areas and then into Old Frisian.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=95}}

[[Calque]]s were common in Old Frisian, especially for Latin terms adopted during the Christianization of the Frisians, such as {{lang|ofs|godeshūs}} ('church', {{lit|God's house}}; Latin {{lang|la|domus Dei}}) and {{lang|ofs|elemechtich}} ('almighty'; Latin {{lang|la|omnipotens}}).{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=96}} Other loan translations include the days of the week and some terms associated with the military or leadership roles, such as {{lang|ofs|hāvedmon}} ('leader, chieftain'; Latin {{lang|la|capitaneus}}){{efn|For the relationship between Old Frisian {{lang|ofs|hāved}} and Latin {{lang|la|caput}}, see {{harvnb|Boutkan|Siebinga|2005|pp=155–156}}.}} and {{lang|ofs|herestrēte}} ('highroad, military road'; Latin {{lang|la|via militaris}}).{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=96}} Alternations between native Old Frisian words and Latin loans bled into legal texts as well, often as glosses or definitions. Examples include in the {{lang|ofs|{{ill|Freeska Landriucht|fy||stq}}}}, where the terms {{lang|ofs|[[Schout|scelta]]}} and {{lang|ofs|[[Asega|aesgha]]}} both are glossed as Latin {{lang|la|iudex}} ('judge, magistrate') even though the duties in Frisian society were not equivalent.{{sfn|Nijdam|Hallebeek|de Jong|2023|pp=25–26}}

==Phonology==
Old Frisian [[phonology]] has been reconstructed by analyzing the existing corpora and the language's modern descendants.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=21}} In general, Old Frisian scribes used a largely [[phonemic orthography]], where each letter signifies a distinct phoneme.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=613}} With limited exceptions, [[stress (linguistics)|stress]] fell on the [[Word stem|stem]], the base form of the word to which affixes may be added, in Old Frisian.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=87}}

