No distinction was made orthographically in early Old Frisian to provide for [[vowel length]], though in later forms of the language an {{angle bracket|e}} or {{angle bracket|i}} was placed after the vowel to indicate a long vowel, as in {{lang|ofs|baem}} ({{IPA|ofs|baːm|}}, {{gloss |tree}}).{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=114}} The vowel {{IPAslink|æ}} may have been a [[marginal phoneme]], where it is found in one extant manuscript as a [[reflex (linguistics)|reflex]] of the same Proto–West Germanic phoneme, though inclusion of {{IPA|/æ/}} is based on very little evidence.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=617}}

The language had two [[diphthong]]s: {{lang|ofs|iā}} and {{lang|ofs|iū}}, the latter of which was interchangeable with {{lang|ofs|iō}}.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=45}}{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=617}} The long vowel in {{lang|ofs|iā}} is assumed from attestations in Old West Frisian, but it is unclear if it was long in earlier forms of the language, as the vowel length is unclear in the older Old East Frisian manuscripts.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=617}} In later Old West Frisian, the traditional {{lang|ofs|iā}} developed into {{IPA|[ɪɛː]}}, usually written as {{lang|ofs|iee}}.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=45}} While there were rising diphthong short forms in {{lang|ofs|iu~io}}, no short {{lang|ofs|*ia}} is attested.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=45}} Other marginal diphthongs existed too. For example, {{lang|ofs|ei}} was common in loanwords, as in {{lang|ofs|keiser}} ('emperor') from Old High German, though it existed in some native terms such as {{lang|ofs|dei}}.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=46}}{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=617}} Later developments include {{IPA|/ai/}}, as in {{lang|ofs|flail}} ('[[flail (weapon)|flail]]').{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=617}}{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=47}} Two consecutive vowels did not always form a diphthong. For example, {{lang|ofs|fīand}} ('enemy') has a syllable break between the first vowel and the second.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=46–47}}

