[[Gemination]], or the long pronunciation of consonants, was possible for most consonants in word-medial position,{{efn|{{harvp|Boutkan|2001}} finds evidence for word-final geminates lacking, but concedes this may be the result of orthographic convention rather than a strict phonological feature.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=615}}}} though [[semivowel]]s (i.e., {{IPA|/w/}} and {{IPA|/j/}}), voiced [[allophone]]s of voiceless fricatives typically found between vowels (i.e., {{IPA|/v/}}, {{IPA|/ð/}}, {{IPA|/z/}}, and {{IPA|/ɣ/}}), and the alveolar affricates were exceptions. [[Dirk Boutkan]] argues that {{IPA|/f/}} was an exception as well, but Bremmer includes it.<ref>{{multiref|{{harvnb|Boutkan|2001|pp=613–615}}.|{{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=47}}.}}</ref>{{efn|{{harvp|Boutkan|2001}} also excludes {{IPA|/x/}}, but appears to contradict himself elsewhere in the same work.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|pp=613–615}}}} In earlier orthographies, geminate consonants were consistently written with duplicated consonants unless they were found in word-final position; later, the duplication only signified that the previous vowel was short.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=21–23, 48}}

The phoneme {{IPA|/x/}} was typically pronounced at the beginning of a syllable as {{IPA|ofs|h|}} if the syllable was stressed and the phoneme occurred before a vowel, {{IPA|/l/}}, {{IPA|/r/}}, or {{IPA|/w/}}.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=614}} It is possible that {{IPA|/xl/}}, {{IPA|/xr/}}, or {{IPA|/xw/}} clusters were realized as the voiceless allophones {{IPA|ofs|l̥|}}, {{IPA|ofs|r̥|}}, or {{IPA|ofs|ʍ|}}, respectively, given that the orthography sometimes swaps them; for example, {{lang|ofs|hl-}} and {{lang|ofs|lh-}} are both attested for the same sequence.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=614}} If true, Old Frisian is unique in its preservation of these voiceless [[liquid consonant]]s among other contemporary Germanic languages.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=614}} In all other cases, {{IPA|ofs|x|}} is believed to have been pronounced.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=614}} The phoneme {{IPA|/g/}} was devoiced and [[Spirantization|spirantized]] in word-final position; in other words, it became {{IPA|[x]}} as well.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=615}}

====Orthographic conventions====
The [[dental fricatives]] {{IPA|/θ/}} and {{IPA|/ð/}} were both written as {{angle bracket|th}}, irrespective of voicing; the phoneme {{IPA|/t/}} was sometimes written as {{angle bracket|th}}, but no pronunciation change is thought to have occurred.<ref>{{multiref2|For the orthography of dental fricatives, see {{harvnb|Boutkan|2001|p=614}}.|For everything else, see {{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=114}}.}}</ref> Similarly, the {{IPA|/sk/}} cluster was sometimes written as {{angle bracket|sch}}, but it was still likely pronounced as {{IPA|[sk]}}. Though {{angle bracket|kh}} was often pronounced {{IPA|ofs|k|}}, {{angle bracket|ch}} could represent either {{IPA|/x/}} or {{IPA|/k/}}, though {{IPA|/k/}} is vastly less common except in loans from [[Medieval Latin]], where it could also represent {{IPA|/x/}}, especially word-initially or before {{angle bracket|t}}.<ref>{{multiref2|For the insertion of {{lang|ofs|h}} after {{lang|ofs|c}} and {{lang|ofs|k}}, see {{harvnb|Bremmer|Neri|Schuhmann|2025|p=75}}.|For orthography of {{IPA|/x/}}, see {{harvnb|Boutkan|2001|p=614}}.|For everything else, see {{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=114}}.}}</ref>{{efn|The use of {{angle bracket|kh}} was rare in Old Frisian, but is attested in forms like {{lang|ofs|khinda}}, the [[genitive]] form of 'children'.{{sfn|Bremmer|Neri|Schuhmann|2025|p=75}}}} Instead, {{angle brackets|ch}} almost always represented either {{IPA|/x/}} or its geminate equivalent.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=614}} The insertion of {{angle bracket|h}} was probably imported from orthographic conventions common among [[Middle Low German]] scribes, though it was relatively rare and found primarily in Latin loans.<ref>{{multiref2|For its relative rarity and its use primarily in loanwords, see {{harvnb|Boutkan|2001|p=614}}.|For everything else, see {{harvnb|Bremmer|Neri|Schuhmann|2025|p=75}}.}}</ref>

The [[digraph (orthography)|digraph]] {{lang|ofs|gh}} was often used to represent {{IPA|ofs|ɣ|}}, the fricative allophone of {{IPA|/g/}} or voiced allophone of {{IPA|/x/}}, but could represent {{IPA|/g/}} as well.<ref>{{multiref2|For the representation of {{IPA|/g/}} with {{angle bracket|g}}, {{harvnb|Bremmer|Neri|Schuhmann|2025|p=75}}.|For {{IPA|[ɣ]}} as an allophone of {{IPA|/x/}}, see {{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=47}}.|For everything else see {{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=114}}.}}</ref> The semivowel {{IPA|/j/}} could be variously represented as {{angle bracket|i}}, {{angle bracket|y}}, {{angle bracket|j}}, or {{angle bracket|g}}, though the latter only occurred before high vowels.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=614}} Both {{angle bracket|g}} and {{angle bracket|h}} could be used unetymologically to mark syllable boundaries; {{angle bracket|g}} typically used for {{IPA|ofs|j|}}, as in {{lang|ofs|wīges}} (genitive singular of 'way') instead of the also attested {{lang|ofs|wīes}}, while {{angle bracket|h}} was silent, as in {{lang|ofs|israhelisk}} ('Israeli').{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=614}} The [[digraph (orthography)|digraph]] {{angle bracket|qu}} was sometimes used for the sequence {{IPA|ofs|kw|}}; both {{lang|ofs|quic}} and {{lang|ofs|kuic}} ('animal'), both pronounced {{IPA|ofs|kwik|}}, are attested in the corpora.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=614}} The sound {{IPA|ofs|v|}} could be represented with either {{angle bracket|v}} or {{angle bracket|u}}.{{sfn|Boutkan|2001|p=614}} In the {{lang|la|Codex Unia}}, a document found west of the Lauwers, the language shows signs of voicing in word-initial fricatives like that found in Middle and Modern Dutch, though this does not persist in Modern West Frisian.<ref>{{multiref2|For the origins of the {{lang|la|Codex Unia}}, see {{harvnb|Johnston|2001|p=577}}.|For everything else, see {{harvnb|Boutkan|2001|p=614}}.}}</ref>

===Dialectal variation===
Old Frisian phonology was not uniform. For example, around the year 1200, the [[Proto–West Germanic]] phoneme {{lang|gem-x-proto|þ}} became {{IPA|/d/}} in word-medial and word-final positions in several Old Frisian dialects.{{efn|Old West Frisian also demonstrates word-initial {{IPA|ofs|d|}} from an earlier {{lang|gem-x-proto|þ}}, such as in Old West Frisian {{lang|ofs|dis}} instead of {{lang|ofs|this}} ('this').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=54–55}}}} This change did not affect Old Weser Frisian or North Frisian and forms like {{lang|ofs|lathia}} existed beside {{lang|ofs|ladia}} in different dialects during the same period.{{sfn|Stiles|2017|p=893}}

====Old Weser Frisian====
Short vowels in unstressed final syllables in Old Weser Frisian were in [[complementary distribution]]; this distribution is called "vowel balance". When the preceding vowel was short and the introduction of vowel balance would cause the additional short vowel to be in an open syllable, {{lang|ofs|i}} or {{lang|ofs|u}} appear, such as in {{lang|ofs|Godi}} ('to God') or {{lang|ofs|skipu}} ('ships').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=112}}{{sfn|Winter|2022|p=6}} If the preceding vowel was long or a diphthong, or if the stem vowel was separated by another syllable, the word ended with the vowels {{lang|ofs|e}} or {{lang|ofs|o}}, such as in {{lang|ofs|liōde}} ('people'). This regular distribution of word-final vowels has allowed linguists to differentiate between long and short vowels in Old Frisian documents where vowel length is not marked.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=112}} For example, the word {{lang|ofs|hōve}} (dative singular of 'hoof'), with a long first vowel, could be distinguished from its short vowel counterpart {{lang|ofs|hovi}} (dative singular of 'court, courtyard').{{sfn|Bremmer|2001|p=603}} The consequences of vowel balance are reflected in two of the descendant dialects, [[Wangerooge Frisian|Wangerooge]] and [[Wursten Frisian|Wursten]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=112}}

Old Weser Frisian also raised {{lang|ofs|e}} to {{lang|ofs|i}} before {{lang|ofs|r}} ({{lang|ofs|irthe}}, 'earth') and raised {{lang|ofs|a}} and {{lang|ofs|u}} to {{lang|ofs|i}} through [[i-mutation|''i''-mutation]] ({{lang|ofs|kining}}, 'king'). However, {{lang|ofs|i}} was lowered to {{lang|ofs|e}} and {{lang|ofs|u}} to {{lang|ofs|o}} in open syllables if the following syllable contains {{lang|ofs|a}}. This last process is known as the Rüstring ''a''-mutation.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=112}} Following [[Fronting (sound change)|fronting]] and the [[Palatalization (sound change)|palatalization]] of {{lang|gem-x-proto|-ag-}} and {{lang|gem-x-proto|-eg}}, which typically became {{lang|ofs|ei}}, Old Weser Frisian exhibits {{lang|ofs|ī}}, such as in {{lang|ofs|dī}} ('day') instead of {{lang|ofs|dei}} and {{lang|ofs|brīn}} ('brain') instead of {{lang|ofs|brein}}. Proto-Germanic {{lang|gem-x-proto|ē₂}} also became {{lang|ofs|ī}}.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=112}}

====Old Ems Frisian====
Old Ems Frisian diphthongized {{lang|ofs|ē}} to {{lang|ofs|ei}} before a voiced [[alveolar consonant]] including [[resonants]], as in {{lang|ofs|breid}} ('bride', also 'broad'). In unstressed syllables, the suffix {{lang|ofs|-en}} inserted {{lang|ofs|r}} between the vowel and the final consonant, such as in {{lang|ofs|wēpern}} ('weapon') instead of {{lang|ofs|wēpen}}. In later forms of the dialect, {{lang|ofs|a}} became lengthened after some consonant clusters; {{lang|ofs|ā}} then had a tendency to become rounded to {{lang|ofs|ō}} ({{IPA|ofs|ɔː|}}) irrespective of if it had been lengthened by the consonant cluster lengthening. This gave rise to forms such as {{lang|ofs|ōlle}} ('all') instead of {{lang|ofs|alle}}, though forms like {{lang|ofs|ōlsa}} ('so') – against the non-Ems form {{lang|ofs|alsā}} – show rounding but not in both circumstances.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=113–114}}

====Old West Frisian====
Orthographic conventions used in Old West Frisian help to make the phonological structure much clearer than those of the Old East Frisian dialects. Vowel length was frequently marked, either with the addition of an {{angle bracket|e}} after the long vowel, as in {{lang|ofs|boek}} ('book'), or the duplication of the long vowel, as in {{lang|ofs|huus}} ('house') or {{lang|ofs|wiif}} ('woman').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=114}} Orthographic duplication of long {{lang|ofs|u}} was sometimes {{angle bracket|uu}} and sometimes {{angle bracket|w}}, as in {{lang|ofs|hws}}. Similarly, a long {{lang|ofs|i}} may sometimes be represented as {{angle bracket|ij}}, as in {{lang|ofs|sijn}} ('his'), or {{angle bracket|y}}, as in {{lang|ofs|lyf}} ('[[wergeld]]').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=114}} In some instances, {{angle bracket|y}} or {{angle bracket|i}} could be used as a length modifier as well, as in {{lang|ofs|teyken}} ('sign') or {{lang|ofs|kuith}} ('known, public').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=114}}

Old West Frisian demonstrated rounding of {{lang|gem-x-proto|a}} before [[nasal consonants]]; this was later constrained to the northeastern dialect before {{lang|ofs|-mn}} or {{lang|ofs|-nn}}, as the southwestern dialect restored it to {{lang|ofs|a}}. When {{lang|ofs|v}} occurred between vowels, it became {{lang|ofs|w}}, as in {{lang|ofs|howe}} instead of {{lang|ofs|hove}} for the dative singular of 'court'; this also sometimes led to the collapse of the two-vowel structure, causing a diphthong to occur, as in {{lang|ofs|hāud}} ('head'; from earlier {{lang|ofs|hāwed}} inherited from {{lang|ofs|hāved}}). This sound change is also found in later forms of the Old East Frisian dialects.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=114}}

Old West Frisian also exhibited several [[vowel breaking]] processes. One is a process called "Jorwert breaking" where long front vowels followed by {{lang|ofs|w}} were converted into rising diphthongs. This means that {{IPA|ofs|iːw|}}, {{IPA|ofs|eːw|}}, and {{IPA|ofs|ɛːw|}} were converted into {{IPA|ofs|juːw|}}, {{IPA|ofs|joːw|}}, and {{IPA|ofs|jɔːw|}}, respectively. Sometimes the {{lang|ofs|j}} was deleted if it followed an {{lang|ofs|r}}.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=114–115}} Before consonant clusters beginning with a [[liquid consonant]], {{lang|ofs|e}} was typically raised to {{lang|ofs|i}}.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=115}} In another process, called "late Old West Frisian breaking", consonant clusters where {{lang|ofs|l}} preceded {{lang|ofs|d}}, {{lang|ofs|k}}, {{lang|ofs|n}}, or {{lang|ofs|r}}, the preceding {{lang|ofs|e}} was lengthened, diphthongized, and stress shifted to the second syllable. This process can be seen in examples such as {{lang|ofs|feld}} lengthening to {{lang|ofs|fēld}} before breaking into {{lang|ofs|fiēld}}; stress originally fell on the first syllable, then shifted to the final syllable.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=115}} Before the cluster {{lang|ofs|nd}}, {{lang|ofs|e}} diphthongized to {{lang|ofs|ei}}. In the sequence {{lang|ofs|-we-}}, both elements merged into {{lang|ofs|-o-}}. The diphthong {{lang|ofs|iā}} raised to {{lang|ofs|iē}}, pronounced as {{IPA|ofs|jɛː|}}. The [[voiceless dental fricative]] {{lang|ofs|th}} became {{lang|ofs|t}} word-initially and the [[voiced dental fricative]], also represented as {{lang|ofs|th}}, became {{lang|ofs|d}} word-initially and -medially. Between vowels, {{lang|ofs|d}} – including those previously dental fricatives – are elided, as in {{lang|ofs|snīa}} ('to cut'; from earlier {{lang|ofs|snītha}}). Word-final {{lang|ofs|d}} was devoiced and {{lang|ofs|u}} was raised to {{lang|ofs|o}} before nasal consonants.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=115}}

==Morphology==
[[File:Freeska Landriucht incipit.png|thumb|The {{lang|ofs|{{ill|Freeska Landriucht|v=ib|fy||stq}}}} (copy shown dated to {{circa|1485}}) is a medieval document with [[Latin]] and Old Frisian law texts.{{efn|Although this corpus of legal texts is referred to as "Old Frisian law texts", at least seven of the texts predate the Old Frisian period.{{sfn|Nijdam|Hallebeek|de Jong|2023|p=15}}}}|alt=A manuscript written in blackletter font; the incipit begins with an ornate letter T which is hastily colored in]]
Old Frisian distinguished between three [[grammatical gender]]s: masculine, feminine, and neuter.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=53}} [[Grammatical case|Case]] appears to have been somewhat variable; while [[nominative case|nominative]], [[accusative case|accusative]], [[genitive case|genitive]], and [[dative case]]s are robustly attested, the [[instrumental case]] was preserved in some [[Fossilization (linguistics)|fossilized]] phrases and a [[locative case]] has been documented in a few attestations.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=53}}{{sfn|de Haan|2010|p=54}} Only two [[grammatical number]]s are attested in Old Frisian (singular and plural), though a [[Dual (grammatical number)|dual number]] is attested in both Insular and Mainland forms of North Frisian, becoming obsolete during the early 20th century. Old Frisian likely had a dual number, but the legal context in which most attestations occur did not give cause for the use of the dual.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=56}} Old Frisian did not have [[reflexive pronoun]]s for most of its history; although the inherited Germanic reflexive {{lang|ofs|sīn}} is attested, it displaced the expected neuter genitive singular pronoun {{lang|ofs|*his}} and the language instead used the accusative case to express the reflexive grammatical function.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=56}}

===Pronouns===
Pronouns in Old Frisian are only attested in four cases: nominative, accusative, genitive, and dative.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=54}} Like other Ingvaeonic languages such as Old English and Old Saxon, there was no distinction between the accusative and dative, which was contrasted with other West Germanic languages like Old High German.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=55}}

