Old West Frisian innovated the second-person plural form {{lang|ofs|iemman}}, sometimes rendered as {{lang|ofs|iemma}}, a [[Univerbation|combined form]] composed of {{lang|ofs|jī}} and {{lang|ofs|man}} (literally 'you men'). This form did not decline for case and {{lang|ofs|jī}} remained the [[T–V distinction|polite form of address]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=55}} Old Frisian had [[clitic]]ized pronouns which were attached to the end of words; their use has made translation more difficult since they were not marked as distinct from other [[homonym]]ic suffixes.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=56}} Possessive pronouns declined like strong adjectives and interrogative pronouns did not decline for grammatical gender. The interrogative pronoun {{lang|ofs|hwet}} ('what') was sometimes marked for number, but only in the accusative and dative forms. The interrogative pronoun {{lang|ofs|hwa}} ('who') was typically pronounced with a short vowel, but pronounced long utterance-finally.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=57}}

Pronominal forms were sometimes used to recapitulate nouns and other pronouns in order to establish clarity. Examples include:{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=107}}

{{Interlinear|number=(a)
|Thi blata '''thi''' is lethost allera nata.
|that {poor man} '''that''' is {most miserable} all-GEN-PL companion-GEN-PL
|'The poor man, he is the most miserable of all companions.'}}

===Nouns===
Old Frisian nouns are classified into three archetypes. Type I are weak/consonant-stemmed nouns, type II are strong/vowel-stemmed nouns, and type III is a catch-all category which mainly comprises other kinds of consonant-stemmed nouns of which the Indo-European reflex had the case marked immediately to the root word.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=58–59}} Masculine words ending in {{nowrap|{{lang|ofs|-a}}}} and feminine or neuter words ending in {{nowrap|{{lang|ofs|-e}}}} are classed in type I, though there were only two neuter words in this type: {{lang|ofs|āre}} ('ear') and {{lang|ofs|āge}} ('eye').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=59}} Type II comprises a wide variety of strong masculine nouns and predominately abstract feminine nouns. The neuter suffix {{lang|ofs|-skipi}} or {{lang|ofs|-skipe}} also governs the type II paradigm, though this is attested as a feminine suffix as well.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=59}} Below is an example of an ''n''-stem declension, a kind of type I declension pattern:

