===Verbs===
{{broader|Germanic verbs}}
Verbs in Old Frisian comprised four types: strong, weak, [[preterite-present]], and anomalous. With few exceptions, the only [[Productivity (linguistics)|productive]] verb declension was the weak paradigm.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=70}} Some [[paradigm leveling]] to weak declensions occurred among strong verbs in later forms of the language.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=86}} The anomalous class of verbs are a composite class comprising [[suppletion|suppletive verbs]], verbs without clear preterite forms, and verbs with defective or missing declension forms.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=81}} 

In general, verbs tended to end in either {{lang|ofs|-a}} or {{lang|ofs|-ia}} with later forms reduced to {{lang|ofs|-e}} or {{lang|ofs|-ie}}, respectively.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=84, 86}} Noteworthy exceptions include {{lang|ofs|gān}} and {{lang|ofs|stān}} in Old West Frisian; this word-final {{lang|ofs|-n}} became more widespread in monosyllabic verbs in later forms of that dialect, such as in {{lang|ofs|dwān}} ('to do') and {{lang|ofs|siān}} ('to see').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=84}} Infinitive forms used the lengthened suffix {{nowrap|{{lang|ofs|-ane}}}} after the word {{lang|ofs|tō}} – used to express purpose – such as in the phrase {{lang|ofs|tō farane}} ('to travel').<ref>{{multiref2|For its use in expressing purpose, see {{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=102}}.|For everything else, see {{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|p=84}}.}}</ref> In Old Weser Frisian and Old Ems Frisian, present participles and [[gerund]]s had identical forms.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=84}} Like [[modern English]], the conjunction {{lang|ofs|thet}} ('that') was sometimes omitted after verbs of expression in some contexts ({{lang|ofs|Tha spreken se hia ne kuden.}} 'Then they said [that] they were unable to.').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=107}}

====Strong verbs====
The infinitive, the first- and third-person singular preterite, the plural preterite, and the past participle are the four constituent parts identifying a strong verb. These verbs are defined by their use of [[vowel gradation]] – including changes to vowel quality or length – to signal a change in meaning, such as person or tense.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=70}} Like nominal declensions, phonological explanations for irregularity are present and similarly do not change classification.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=74}} There were six classes of strong verbs in Old Frisian with a seventh catch-all category.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=71, 74}} Classes IV and V became functionally identical after ''i''-mutation, a morphophonological change which obfuscated the differentiation between the historical {{lang|gem-x-proto|i}} and {{lang|gem-x-proto|u}} in some contexts, and are distinguished only by historical provenance.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=74}} Examples of verbal paradigms can be seen below:
