====Weak verbs====
Unique to Old Frisian, there were only two weak verb classes; [[Gothic language|Gothic]] had twice as many, while Old Norse, Old English, Old Saxon, and Old High German each had three.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=78}} Class I weak verbs comprised verbs which originally had a suffix, {{Lang|gem-x-proto|-jan}}, which created causative verbs from strong verb stems and factitive verbs from nouns and adjectives, such as {{Lang|ofs|dēma}} ('to judge') from {{Lang|ofs|dōm}} ('judgement'). [[Morphophonological]]ly, the {{Lang|gem-x-proto|j}} affected consonants through [[assibilation]] and the vowels through [[Umlaut (linguistics)|mutation]].{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=78–79}} Class I weak verbs have the past tense suffix {{nowrap|{{Lang|ofs|-de}}}}, or {{Lang|ofs|-te}} after voiceless consonants. Geminated consonants became singletons in the preterite and past participles.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=79}} By contrast, class II weak verbs are typically those which end in {{Lang|ofs|-ia}}. These verbs had their past tense marked by the deletion of the {{Lang|ofs|i}} and the addition of the suffix {{Lang|ofs|-ade}}; the past participle was formed with the same deletion and a simple {{Lang|ofs|-ad}} suffix. Later forms of the suffixes were {{Lang|ofs|-ede}} and {{Lang|ofs|-ed}}, respectively. In late Old West Frisian, these past tense suffixes were deleted.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=80}} Class II has remained productive into the modern period; Frisian is the only branch of West Germanic languages to have preserved this class of verbs.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=80}}

====Preterite-present====
{{further|Preterite-present}}
Germanic languages have a verb class in which a form resembling a past-tense strong verb supplies the present-tense meaning while the past-tense form is re-formed with a weak verbal suffix; infinitive forms are also formed through innovation.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=80}} These verbs exhibit expected vowel alternations for strong verbs for some forms while other forms are in line with expected weak verb declensions.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=80}} In Old Frisian, they are categorized into one of the six strong verb classes the strong verb form is derived from.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=81}}

==Syntax==

===Case===
Case did not vary much in Old Frisian when compared to other contemporary Germanic languages. The nominative case was used for the subjects or subject complements though it was also used in [[Vocative case|vocative]] contexts.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=98}} While the main use of the accusative was to mark the [[direct object]] of a verb, it was also used in temporal and spatial expressions, such as mentioning spaces of time ({{lang|ofs|niugen monath}}, 'nine months') or distances ({{lang|ofs|Hi gunge tha niugen heta skera.}} 'He should walk the nine hot plowshares.').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=98}} Genitive usage was complex and multifaceted; it marked possession and relationships, but was also used to mark adverbs and had both [[Partitive case|partitive]] and numerical functions including measures ({{lang|ofs|tha wi sigun hundred folkes santon}}, 'when we sent seven hundred [armed] men') and counting ({{lang|ofs|thritich fethma}}, 'thirty fathoms').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=98–99}}

The dative case was also complex. Although it marked the indirect object of a [[ditransitive verb]], it was sometimes used for the direct objects of transitive verbs, such as {{lang|ofs|helpa}} ('to help').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=99}} The dative shared some overlap in function with the genitive, including its use in adverbial phrases and measurements. Dative constructions were also used to mark the [[benefactive case|benefactive]], such as in the sentence {{lang|ofs|God him reste}} ('God rested [for himself]').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=99}} A number of adjectives governed the dative as well, typically marking either physical or emotional closeness.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=99}}

As the case system began to break down in Old Frisian, authors – especially those of legal documents – came to rely heavily on word order and changed the use of prepositions. By late Old Frisian, case marking was optional.{{sfn|de Haan|2010|pp=54–55}}

===Verbs===
Old Frisian marked for two [[Grammatical tense|tenses]] in the verbal root: simple present and simple past, also called the simple preterite. All other tenses, called compound tenses, were expressed through [[periphrasis]] using [[auxiliary verb]]s. While these tenses were not common in earlier forms of the language, they became more popular over time.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=103}} 

Compound tenses used the auxiliaries meaning 'to have' ({{lang|ofs|hebba}} in Old East Frisian, {{lang|ofs|habba}} in Old West Frisian) and 'to be' ({{lang|ofs|wesa}}). The use of {{lang|ofs|hebba}}/{{lang|ofs|habba}} and the past participle were used to express the [[past perfective]] and less commonly the [[pluperfect]]. These usages were largely constrained to [[dependent clause]]s.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=103–104}} The use of {{lang|ofs|wesa}} is less clear, but it appears to have been used as somewhat of a [[present progressive]] when in combination with a present participle. It is often difficult to differentiate between a progressive semantic meaning or a [[copula (linguistics)|copula]]r relationship. Particularly with [[verbs of motion]], {{lang|ofs|wesa}} was also used in some intransitive contexts to express the perfect or pluperfect to express changes in state.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=104}} The perfect of {{lang|ofs|wesa}} was used with {{lang|ofs|hebba}}/{{lang|ofs|habba}}, though this was uncommon in earlier forms of the language. 

The [[passive voice]] was typically constructed with the verb {{lang|ofs|wertha}} ('to become') and the past participle, though {{lang|ofs|wesa}} and the past participle could be used to form a perfective passive.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=104–105}} The combined use of {{lang|ofs|wesa}} and the present participle were used for the [[durative aspect]], while the [[future tense]] used the combination of the auxiliary {{lang|ofs|skela}} and the infinitive.{{sfn|de Haan|2010|p=52}} Non-auxiliary verbs, such as {{lang|ofs|biginna}} ('to begin') and {{lang|ofs|gunga}} ('to go'), were used with the infinitive to express an [[inchoative aspect]]. Similarly, verbs like {{lang|ofs|dwā}} ('to do') and {{lang|ofs|lēta}} ('to let') were used to form the [[causative]].<ref>{{multiref2|For the definition of these terms and the long vowels, see {{harvnb|Bremmer|2009|pp=77, 82}}.|For their syntactic function, see {{harvnb|de Haan|2010|p=52}}.}}</ref>

The language also marked for three [[Grammatical mood|moods]] in the root: [[indicative]] for statements of fact or observations, [[subjunctive]] for subjective thoughts including guesswork and conjecture, and [[imperative mood|imperative]] for commands.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=102}} The indicative and subjunctive moods may be used next to each other in different clauses of the same sentence.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=102}} The infinitive was used in several ways, but the inflected infinitive – an infinitive preceded by {{lang|ofs|tō}} – operated as a gerund. This inflected form was used to express purpose and sentences containing it would often drop the subject and the associated finite verb. A unique construction using the uninflected infinitive, called the accusative-plus-infinitive construction, was sometimes used as a complement, as in {{lang|ofs|tha segen hia anne thretundista sitta}} ('then they saw sitting a thirteenth [man]').{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|pp=102–103}}

===Word order===
Word order in Old Frisian varied widely depending on context and function. The language's [[Word order|constituent word order]] is generally described as [[subject–object–verb]] (SOV).{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=105}}{{sfn|de Haan|2010|pp=47, 49}} Dependent clauses strongly tend towards this word order as well, though some departures from this trend are attested.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=106}} However, [[Corpus linguistics|analysis of the existing corpora]] involving charter documents shows that about 60% of dependent sentences with direct objects had a [[subject–verb–object]] (SVO) construction.{{sfn|de Haan|2010|p=49}} [[Object–verb–subject]] (OVS) constructions were commonly employed as a method of [[topicalization]], wherein the object of the sentence was emphasized over elements of the sentence.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=105}} Both [[Conditional sentence|conditional]] and [[interrogative clause]]s were typically [[verb–subject–object]] (VSO).{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=105}} Dependent conditional clauses used [[object–subject–verb]] (OSV) constructions as well when interrogative pronouns are in grammatical cases other than the nominative.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=106}}

In contexts using a case other than the nominative, pronouns could be moved between the verb and the subject when the subject was in a later position than the verb, leading to a [[verb–object–subject]] (VOS) word order.{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=106}} This word order is completely absent in modern Frisian.{{sfn|de Haan|2010|p=54}} Examples of this include the following:{{sfn|Bremmer|2009|p=106}}

{{Interlinear
| number = (b)|tha het '''se''' thi koning alle heran|then called '''them''' the king all lords|'then the king called them all lords'
}}

Like all other Germanic languages at some point in their history, Old Frisian exhibited properties of [[verb-second word order]] (V2), though its application was inconsistent.{{sfn|de Haan|2010|pp=49–50, 53}} This means that the verb appears in the second position in [[independent clause]]s with a finite verb, but reverts to verb-final word order in subordinate clauses.{{sfn|Roberts|2023|p=166}}

Old Frisian sentences almost always required a subject and the language often employed the use of [[dummy subject]]s. This appears to be a syntactic necessity even when there was not semantic function. Examples include verbs involving the weather and impersonal passives, respectively demonstrated below:{{sfn|de Haan|2010|p=53}}

{{Interlinear
| number = (c)|hwant '''hit''' wayt ende stormit alle daghen|for '''it''' blows and storms all days|'for it blows and storms all days'
}}

{{Interlinear
| number = (d)|hwersa'''r''' fuchten is in tha godes huse|{when '''there'''} fought is in the god-GEN house|'when there has been fought in the house of God'
}}

===Negation===
In Old Frisian, negative sentences could be derived from the simple addition of a negative element, such as {{lang|ofs|naet}} ('not') or {{lang|ofs|nimmen}} ('nobody'), or [[double negative]] constructions.{{sfn|de Haan|2010|p=55}} While there was a preference in the language for double negatives, all three stages of [[Jespersen's cycle]] are present in the existing corpora, though neither of the two Rüstringer codices – the two oldest codices – exhibit the last stage.{{sfn|de Haan|2010|p=57}} The negative marker {{lang|ofs|ne}} preceded the finite verb in both kinds of constructions. Examples include:{{sfn|de Haan|2010|p=55}}

{{Interlinear
| number = (e)|truch thet hia '''ne''' mughen cuma|through that they '''NEG''' may come|'through that, they may not come'
}}

{{Interlinear
| number = (f)|thet hi ter '''nauuet''' cuma '''ne''' machte|that he there '''not''' come '''NEG''' might|'that he might not come there'
}}

The negative marker {{lang|ofs|ne}} often cliticized to the following auxiliary, such as in {{lang|ofs|nabba}} ('to not have'; from {{lang|ofs|ne}} + {{lang|ofs|habba}}) and {{lang|ofs|nis}} ('is not'; from {{lang|ofs|ne}} + {{lang|ofs|is}}). In sentences where the finite verb was elided, the negative marker was also elided and no words or any affixes could come between them. For these reasons, the negative marker and the verb are seen as a unified syntactic unit, with {{lang|ofs|ne}} serving the function of a syntactic clitic.{{sfn|de Haan|2010|pp=55–57}} This was not the case for other negative elements, such as {{lang|ofs|naet}}, which could be divided by other syntactic functions. Contrastive examples of this are demonstrated below, both from the {{lang|ofs|[[Skeltana Riucht]]}}:{{sfn|de Haan|2010|p=56}}

{{Interlinear
| number = (g)|dat hi dine kempa winna '''ne''' mey|that he the champion defeat '''NEG''' may|'that he may not defeat the champion'
}}

{{Interlinear
| number = (h)|ief hi dine kempa '''naet''' winna mey|if he the champion '''not''' defeat may|'if he may not defeat the champion'
}}

In sentences where the only verb was a finite verb in a main clause, the use of {{lang|ofs|naet}} was mostly restricted to the sentence-final position, but in subordinate clauses with double negatives, {{lang|ofs|naet}} was promoted to the position before {{lang|ofs|ne}}.{{sfn|de Haan|2010|p=56}}

